  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather continues today, though a few \nvery isolated instability/sea breeze showers will occur this \nafternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure ridges across the area begins slowly \nmoving offshore tonight. The high is relatively weak and with \nthe warm and relatively humid airmass, there could be a few \npop- up showers across the sea- breeze fronts thru the rest of \nthe afternoon. Coverage on Friday was rather sparse, and it \nappears it will be that way again today. Clouds and southerly\nflow tonight will promote a milder night, with lows mainly in\nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. Sunday will be notably more humid,\nwith most areas seeing dew points creep back into the low-mid \n70s and actual temps again in the mid-upper 80s. This will \nresult in a day that feels warmer despite relatively minimal \nsunshine under thickening high and mid clouds. The next system \napproaching may bring some showers and storms into the area \nlate, but most likely hold off until evening, so have generally \nheld POPs below likely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms increase Sunday night\nand Monday with the potential for areas of heavy rain with some\nflash flooding risk.\n\nAn upper-level trough will shift across eastern Canada this\nweekend and early next week. While this occurs, strong energy \ncrests a ridge in the West and this carves the trough out more \ninto the Tennessee and Ohio valleys by later this weekend. This \nmay result in the trough becoming closed off into early next \nweek as it gradually approaches the East. The main trough may \ndetach and depart to the northeast leaving behind a lingering \nclosed low. This scenario would lead to the system slowing its \neastward progression through the first half of next week. The \nmodel trends continue to slow the arrival of this system, with \nthe bulk of the showers and thunderstorms occurring later Sunday\nnight and especially on Monday.\n\nAhead of the strengthening upper-level trough, deep south to \nsouthwest flow will increase and this will ramp up the low-level\nmoisture and temperature advection. It will therefore be a \nhumid time frame as the precipitable waters surge to 2+ inches. \nThere may be multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms, \nstarting with an initial one later Sunday night into Monday with\nthe increased warm air advection regime. Instability will \nincrease, however the timing at night should limit the \nthunderstorm intensity especially since it may be more of \nelevated convection initially. Areas of heavy rain however will \nbe probable given the increasing forcing for ascent and high \nwater content air. The WPC has included much of our region in a \nMarginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall for Sunday night into \nMonday. On Monday, ripples of energy along an incoming cold \nfront during may enhance the convective potential and with deep \nsoutherly flow, some training convection will be possible. \nExcessive rainfall rates would enhance the flash flooding risk, \nespecially from the I-95 corridor to the north and west where \nthe elevation rise could boost the ascent some given a southeast\nto south low-level flow. There is a Slight Risk (level 2 out of\n4) for Excessive rainfall exists for Monday and Monday night \nfor most of our eastern Pennsylvania counties, most of New \nJersey and into northern Delaware. There is a Marginal Risk \n(level 1 out of 4) for the rest of our area. If enough heating \ndevelops during the course of Monday, that combined with some \nincrease in shear (some initially backed low- level flow) could \nlead to at least some severe thunderstorm risk. This potential \nis less certain though given the chance for multiple rounds of \nconvection/cloud cover, and a moist atmospheric profile which \ntends to limit the overall instability.\n\nGiven the slow eastward progression of this system, the chances\nfor convection look to continue for multiple days next week as \nthe cold front may stall in our vicinity. The current forecast \ndoes have shower and thunderstorm chances each day next week, \nmainly in the afternoon and evening. The most active day, as of \nnow, however looks to be Monday given the overlap of favorable \nparameters for more widespread convective development.",
